WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:03.720
If I asked you to close your eyes
and imagine some wildlife

00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:06.320
you would probably conjure up
an image similar to this… 

00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:09.600
Or even this…  

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:13.680
The focus of your visualisation was
most likely the cute little fox, 

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:18.200
rather than the Digitalis plant
or the Conium Maculatum plant.

00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:20.280
Well, you’re not alone in this, 

00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:23.320
a 2016 study showed
that study participants

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:27.400
remember pictures of animals better
than they remember images of plants. 

00:00:33.080 --> 00:00:34.640
We've never been at this point

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:38.280
of disconnection and estrangement
from the natural world before. 

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:47.760
We're surrounded by synthetic
and electronic entertainment

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:51.560
and we don't interact with plants
and nature as we once would have

00:00:51.640 --> 00:00:55.400
had we been growing our own food
or hunting for our own food. 

00:00:55.480 --> 00:01:00.800
And this has resulted in what is
known as the nature deficit disorder,

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:04.000
where people don't recognise
plants at all

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:06.920
other than something that
they may consume for dinner.  

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:26.120
The human eye sees green
a lot easier than any other colour

00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:29.080
so it tends to group
green objects together

00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:31.800
and fade them into the background. 

00:01:31.880 --> 00:01:35.880
And then there's the evolutionary
development within humans

00:01:35.960 --> 00:01:38.400
that early humans
were threatened by animals

00:01:38.480 --> 00:01:40.480
We also had to look out for movement,

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:42.880
for a fast moving lion
coming to eat us. 

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:47.960
Therefore, the human eye picks up
movement and looks for movement.

00:01:48.040 --> 00:01:51.680
So we often don't recognise plants
which are relatively unmoving.

00:02:02.440 --> 00:02:06.040
Connecting with nature affects us
from our heads to our toes, 

00:02:06.680 --> 00:02:09.240
from micro bacteria in the soil,

00:02:09.320 --> 00:02:12.120
which studies show can
enhance wellbeing, 

00:02:12.440 --> 00:02:16.160
to petrichor, the smell of the earth
after it's rained,

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:18.440
to the sound of birdsong,

00:02:18.520 --> 00:02:21.720
which has been found to decrease
the stress hormone cortisol.

00:02:22.040 --> 00:02:25.520
I realised I was disconnected
from the natural world

00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:27.160
about eight years ago 

00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:32.200
during a period of recovery from
depression and addiction issues. 

00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:37.280
I found myself drawn to walking daily
on Walthamstow marshes. 

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:41.480
And the experience of being in nature
and walking on the marshes 

00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:44.720
became as important
and as therapeutic

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:47.880
as the medication, the psychotherapy

00:02:47.960 --> 00:02:49.760
and the support of friends
and family.

00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:05.440
Those who have less access
to outdoor space,

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:08.200
and two and a half million
people in Britain

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:12.240
don't live within easy, 10 minute
walking distance of a green space ,

00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:16.520
have fewer opportunities
to decrease their stress

00:03:16.600 --> 00:03:19.400
through contact and connection
with the natural world.

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:30.000
Every year, more plant species
go extinct than animal species.

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:32.800
And in fact, CITES,

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:37.120
which is the Convention
on International Trade

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:40.800
in Endangered Wild Species,
that's fauna and flora,

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:44.200
has 30,000 plant species listed

00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:46.640
compared to the 5,000 animal species

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:50.360
which are in danger of extinction
from legal and illegal trade. 

00:03:50.440 --> 00:03:53.120
Plants and fungi are the foundation

00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:56.040
on which humans and
animals really depend.

00:03:56.120 --> 00:03:58.640
And yet they're not recognised
and they are the underdogs.

00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:01.200
They just keep toiling away
and keeping us alive.

00:04:01.280 --> 00:04:04.320
You don't need anything
to go out in nature.

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:07.680
You just need to find something
that you're interested or curious in.

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:10.880
I love insects and lichen and moss.

00:04:10.960 --> 00:04:15.000
I'm always looking around for
different species I can find

00:04:15.080 --> 00:04:16.680
and patterns and shapes.

00:04:16.760 --> 00:04:21.960
So I'd say just find something that
makes you feel wonder and awe

00:04:22.040 --> 00:04:26.400
and say, "Wow," and just continue
your lines of curiosity.

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:29.560
Plants are essential to us.

00:04:29.640 --> 00:04:31.280
They're not just a green background

00:04:31.360 --> 00:04:33.800
or something for us
or animals to eat.

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:39.760
They are significant in our survival
as a species and as a planet. 

